{"question": "The main vessel involved in bleeding from Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma:", "options": {"A": "Facial aery", "B": "Ascending pharyngeal aery", "C": "Internal maxillary aery", "D": "Anterior ethmoidal aery"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Otolaryngology", "Head and Neck Surgery", "Radiology", "Hematology", "Pathology"], "option_domains": ["Vascular Anatomy", "Head and Neck Surgery"], "question_analyses": {"Otolaryngology": "Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (JNA) is a benign but locally aggressive vascular tumor that primarily affects adolescent males. It arises in the nasal cavity and can extend into the paranasal sinuses and nasopharynx. The most alarming aspect of JNA, especially in the context of bleeding, is the involvement of the internal carotid artery (ICA). The internal carotid artery is a major vessel that supplies blood to the brain and is located in close proximity to the nasopharynx. In cases of JNA, the tumor can grow and invade the carotid canal or the cavernous sinus, leading to significant hemorrhage. This bleeding can be life-threatening due to the high blood flow through the ICA. Key points to consider in this scenario include:\n1. **Risk of Severe Hemorrhage**: The involvement of the ICA can lead to massive and potentially fatal bleeding. This is a critical concern as it can rapidly lead to hypovolemic shock and death if not managed promptly. 2. **Complex Surgical Management**: Due to the proximity of the tumor to vital structures, surgical resection is challenging and requires meticulous planning and expertise. The risk of injury to the ICA during surgery is high, necessitating the use of advanced imaging techniques and possibly embolization to reduce blood loss. 3..", "Head and Neck Surgery": "Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (JNA) is a benign but locally aggressive vascular tumor that primarily affects adolescent males. It arises from the nasopharynx and can extend into adjacent structur